**(a)** The monthly Sharpe ratio is $1.0/3.0 = 0.333$. Annualised, it is $0.333\sqrt{12} = 1.15$. The record is $T = 3$ years, so $t = 1.15\sqrt 3 = 2.00$. That equals $\bar r/(s/\sqrt n) = 1.0/(3.0/6)$, as it should. The one-sided p-value is $1 - \Phi(2) = 0.023$. This assumes independent monthly returns and uses the normal approximation.

**(b)** $t_\alpha = 0.6/0.4 = 1.5$. The 95% interval is $0.6 \pm 2.03 \times 0.4$, about $-0.21\%$ to $1.41\%$ a month. It contains 0, so the data cannot rule out zero alpha. Much of the 1% a month is market exposure, with beta 0.9. Not significant is not the same as no skill: 36 months give the test little power.

**(c)** Picking the best of 12 inflates the evidence. The Bonferroni p-value is $12 \times 0.023 = 0.27$. Equivalently, $t = 2.00$ falls short of the bar of 2.64. If all 12 had no edge, the chance that at least one reached $p \le 0.023$ is $1 - 0.977^{12} = 0.24$. The rule of thumb for the best of $N$ noise strategies, $\sqrt{2\ln N}/\sqrt T = \sqrt{2\ln 12}/\sqrt 3 = 1.29$, is above her 1.15. Luck alone explains this record.\n\n**(d)** We need $1.15\sqrt T \ge 2.64$, so $T \ge (2.64/1.15)^2 = 5.2$ years. That is about two more years of the same Sharpe ratio.\n\n**(e)** Ask for:
- the full records of all 12 strategies, and any that were closed, to see the whole search;
- the live record since the fund was chosen, which is out of sample;
- audited returns net of fees and costs, since the pitch figures may be gross;
- how many parameters were tuned, which adds to the hidden number of tries;
- a regression on more factors, such as size, value and momentum, to see if the alpha survives;
- the residuals' autocorrelation, since smoothed marks on illiquid assets inflate the Sharpe ratio and need HAC errors.

Verdict: interesting but unproven. The record is consistent with luck once the search is counted.
